  "notes": "Oxnard, and the loosest hand on this axis. Three parameters carry it and all three are documented. First, key: the research says the sources are \"unpredictable and frequently off-concert-pitch\", so `session.keys` is all twelve at equal weight — no other model on this axis refuses to have a tonal centre preference — and `melody.timbreHint` is `detuned_piano` for the same reason. Second, quantize: hits placed by hand and un-quantised gives 0.55, the lowest here, with the largest timing jitter. Third, arrangement: \"multiple beat switches per track\" and \"very short tracks\" are `switchUpProb` 0.6 and an 8–16 bar verse in hookless structures. ⚠ `drums.hihat.continuous` is false because the research says the hats are irregular; `fillDensity` is 0.34 rather than lower because a model that declares a hat block owes every seed a hat. ⚠ `medium` because the research rates it high-medium: the gear and engineering approach are documented, the drum probabilities are not. The specific inferred parameter is `kick.syncopation` — \"kick around 1 and 3.5\" with everything hand-placed gives no number. ⚠ The i–VII family is INFERRED; the research names static modal vamps and i–iv only, and it carries the lowest weight. ⚠ **The 1-bar phrase length was removed** (was [1,2,4], now [2,4]). A one-bar phrase looped four times carries less entropy than a four-bar phrase, and it is the countermelody it strangles hardest: a counter answers *inside* the melody’s phrase, so a one-bar shape leaves it nowhere to go. Every worst offender on `counter.rs` carried it. ⛔ The previous session recorded this exact mistake and it arrived again from the other direction — distinctness is combinatorial in the shape LENGTH, not in the option count.",
